{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788973211,"nonce":"naXf16S0pBSccV+/IDvG52XSw5g=","message":"IMPLEMENTED on branch `worktree-agent-a921790e5b2c2053d` — `84938e3` (source) and `03a4484` (Build, Release, built at 84938e3). Nothing has been in front of a game; `accept/eb35e1a-nepo-restock.md` carries the bench checks as a numbered list, phase 7 being the acceptance bullet that needs the clock.\n\n=====================================================================\nTHE SCOPING PASS'S FIVE CORRECTIONS, EACH VERIFIED\n=====================================================================\n1. **`RestockOrder.color` is `UnityEngine.Color?`** — confirmed, `RestockOrder.cs`. Bound as `PubField(restock, \"color\", typeof(Color?), …)`; a `ColorDef` binding would have read as drift.\n2. **`SetRestock`'s last two parameters are optional** — confirmed, and moot as predicted: the verb only ever calls `NepoSync.SetRestockRule(ThingDef,int,int,bool,ThingDef,string)`, six params, none optional. `SetRestock` is not bound at all.\n3. **`AllRestocks` is the live `ValueCollection`** — confirmed, `public IEnumerable\u003cRestockOrder\u003e AllRestocks =\u003e restockOrders.Values;`, no memo. The bridge snapshots into its own `List\u003cobject\u003e` (the `PendingShipments` pattern) and the verb never assigns to a row.\n4. **`RunRestockSweep` is private, hourly, `DadAvailable`-gated** — confirmed, and `RestockCheckIntervalTicks` is a **`const int`**, not `static readonly` like `DeliveryDelayTicks`. Reflection reads a literal fine (only Nepo's own callers get it inlined), so it is bound OPTIONALLY, the way the delivery delay is: losing it costs the number, not the verb. The reply's `sweep` block carries `interval_ticks`, `next_sweep_tick` and a `waiting` sentence saying how far to advance.\n5. **A zeroed rule is STORED, not removed** — confirmed. `SetRestock(def, 0, 0, …)` stores `threshold 0 / target 1`. No delete mode; `remove`/`delete`/`clear`/`unset`/`drop`/`cancel` are refused BY NAME with that reasoning, read off the RAW argument dictionary rather than through `VerbArgs.Has` (a `Has` would mark them read and let a later refactor slip them past `RefuseStray`).\n\nAdditions A, B, E, F, G, H implemented as ruled: `EncodeColor` (public static) does the encoding; `ToggleRestockRule` is disclosed but not bound or reproduced; the home map is named on every reply; the restock surface has its own `restockDriftWhy`/`RestockAvailable` tier covering BOTH halves (a renamed `AllRestocks` must not cost `nepo-catalog` either); `NepoGate` grew `requireDad` so `dad-unavailable` is reported and not enforced; the read-back compares five fields post-clamp.\n\nAddition C's four widget gates are all reproduced and cited by member, and are the bulk of the work as predicted.\n\n=====================================================================\nONE CLAIM IN THE SCOPING PASS IS WRONG\n=====================================================================\n**Addition D overstates the `SetMaterialPref` cross-effect.** \"Setting a restock rule silently changes the material a MANUAL `nepo-order` of that def will arrive in\" — it does not. Measured: the ONLY reader of `materialPrefs` anywhere in Nepo is `Window_DadCatalog.SeedCustomizationFromPrefs`, and even there a standing RULE wins over the pref (`rule != null ? rule.stuff : pref.stuff`). `NepoSync.PlaceOrder` WRITES prefs and never reads them; it ships the manifest it is handed, and `nepo-order` always sends an explicit `stuff`. So the real effect is on what a HUMAN's next catalog visit comes up pre-filled with — which is still a real cross-verb effect worth publishing, and it is published on every reply, but the sentence naming `nepo-order` as the victim would have been a false claim in the result.\n\n=====================================================================\nFOUR DECISIONS THE SPEC DID NOT COVER\n=====================================================================\n* **The colour needs a NAME.** The palette is a `List\u003cColor\u003e` and the field is a raw `Color?`, so nothing on that surface is typeable by an agent. `color` is passed as a **ColorDef defName**, resolved through `DefDatabase\u003cColorDef\u003e`, checked against `StylingStationColors()` with Unity's approximate `==` (`List\u003cColor\u003e.Contains` uses EXACT float equality and would reject a legal colour), and reported back as `color_def` + `#RRGGBBAA`. The single-def read lists the whole palette by defName so the agent can pick one.\n* **An absent `stuff`/`color` is CARRIED FORWARD, not sent as null.** `SetRestockRule` has no optionals, so every write sends all six values; treating an absent key as \"clear\" would silently change what the next reorder ships in. A JSON `null` clears; `stuff_source`/`color_source` say which happened. `enabled` likewise defaults to the rule's current state on an update and `true` on a create — the widget exactly (number fields re-send `active`, only the toggle creates).\n* **A create needs BOTH numbers.** The panel stages `stackLimit/2` and `stackLimit` for a def with no rule, but a player reads those before clicking and an agent would not (`c519477`). Refused as `incomplete-rule` rather than defaulted.\n* **The comms-console chain is NOT reproduced**, and this is recorded as a decision rather than an omission. Reaching `DrawRestockPanel` means the catalog window is open, i.e. a sighted colonist walked to a powered console. It is not reproduced because editing a rule charges nothing and ships nothing, and the act it schedules is re-gated on `DadAvailable` at sweep time and, under `requireCommsForRestock`, on a colonist walking to a console to call it in. The difference is WHEN a rule may be written, not WHAT it can buy. `nepo-order`, which charges and ships, keeps the whole chain.\n\n=====================================================================\nONE RED-ERROR VECTOR THE SPEC DID NOT SEE\n=====================================================================\n`ColonyAvailableCount` → `MapCount` → `Verse/ListerThings.ThingsOfDef`, which **`Log.ErrorOnce`s on `MinifiedThing`** and tells the caller to ask for the group instead — and `nepo-restock {def:\"MinifiedThing\"}` is one word away from every other read. The count is computed inside Nepo, so this verb cannot route around the call the way `SpatialVerbs.Nearest` and `things` do: it declines to ask, answers `available: null`, and carries `available_note` saying why. Null is \"not asked\", never \"none\". Phase 1 step 9 of the accept file checks the log afterwards.","files":null}]}